Yes, $f(X)$ is closed in $X$.

**Proof.** Since $f\circ f = f$, the image $f(X)$ coincides with the fixed-point set of $f$. Indeed:
- If $y\in f(X)$, write $y=f(x)$; then $f(y)=f(f(x))=f(x)=y$, so $y$ is a fixed point.
- Conversely, any fixed point $x=f(x)$ lies in $f(X)$ by definition.

Thus $f(X)=\{x\in X: f(x)=x\}$.

Now consider the continuous map
$$g:X\to X\times X,\qquad g(x)=(x,f(x)),$$
which is continuous because its two coordinate maps ($\mathrm{id}_X$ and $f$) are continuous. Since $X$ is Hausdorff, the diagonal $\Delta=\{(x,x):x\in X\}$ is closed in $X\times X$. Therefore
$$f(X)=\{x\in X:f(x)=x\}=g^{-1}(\Delta)$$
is closed in $X$ as the preimage of a closed set under a continuous map.

Hence $f(X)$ is closed in $X$.

